  "result": "At the edge of a small town stood a library that opened only when it rained. No sign explained the arrangement. Under clear skies, its green door remained shut, while the librarian watered ferns on the porch and watched the empty road.\n\nWhen clouds gathered, people arrived carrying umbrellas, damp collars, and private worries. Inside, the rooms smelled of paper, cedar, and wet wool. The shelves held ordinary books beside stranger volumes: manuals for repairing apologies, atlases of forgotten dreams, biographies of people who had almost become famous. Children preferred the basement, where stories rearranged themselves whenever thunder sounded.\n\nThe librarian, Mara, believed rain loosened the knots in people's minds. On dry days, everyone hurried past one another, protected by sunlight and schedules. During storms, they lingered. A baker read poetry beside a mechanic. Two sisters, silent for years, shared a table near the window. An old man returned a novel he had borrowed before his wife died, then checked it out again.\n\nOne autumn afternoon, the town endured a drought. Weeks passed without a drop. Mara unlocked the library anyway. At first, nobody came. Then a boy entered with a glass jar. He placed it on the desk. Inside was a single tear.\n\n\u201cDoes this count?\u201d he asked.\n\nMara smiled, turned the key in the door, and switched on every lamp. By evening, the library was full. Outside, the road stayed dusty, but inside, people spoke softly, listened carefully, and made enough weather for one another that night, at last.",
